<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jianzhou Jurchens</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jianzhou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> were one of the three main groups into which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> divided the Jurchen peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, living in the mountains east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. From 1583 their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> united the Jianzhou and then the other Jurchen groups, organising them into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a> and founding the Later Jin in 1616. In 1635 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> renamed the Jurchens <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, and the state became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens.jpg" alt="Jianzhou Jurchens" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Herrmann Creator:Q1509115 &amp; Georg Westermann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ming_Chinese_empire_1415_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the name Jianzhou is contested: Xu Zhongsha derived it from the region of Parhae, while Japanese scholars hold that it came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> who migrated near the present border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. The Jianzhou Jurchens descended in part from the Huligai, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> classed apart from the Jurchens who founded the Jin dynasty, and from the Taowen and Wodolian tribes of the Yilan area. In 1388 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> enlisted three tribes of Ilan Tumen against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. Ahacu, chief of the Huligai, became commander of the Jianzhou Guard in 1403, and Möngke Temür of the Odori led the Jianzhou Left Guard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The two guards traded with the Ming at Kaiyuan and Fushun and fought the Wild Jurchens to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> to the south; Jurchen raids brought joint Korean and Ming counterattacks in 1467 and 1478 that badly weakened them. By the mid-16th century the guard system had largely disappeared and the Jianzhou formed a confederation of five tribes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a>, the Suksuhu River tribe, Hunehe, Wanggiya, Donggo and Jecen. In the fighting at Fort Gure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>'s grandfather Giocangga and father Taksi were killed alongside Wang Gao's son Atai.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, who claimed descent from Möngke Temür, took over the Suksuhu River tribe, saw his enemy Nikan Wailan killed in 1586, and in 1588 brought the Wanggiya and Donggo together. He captured Fushun in 1618, and after war broke out with the Ming in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> in 1621 his forces won at Guangning and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Jianzhou were rich in natural resources and kept trade secrets in curing ginseng and dyeing cloth, sold at the licensed market of Fushun with horses. They took up farming under the Ming, learning to use fertiliser, draft animals and iron ploughs, and they acquired iron-smelting from 1599. The Korean envoy Sin Chun-li, sent in winter to ransom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> seized in 1594, found villages of twenty households or fewer along forested riverbanks. They used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, Mongolian and Chinese, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> script devised under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> was at first little used.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jianzhou Jurchens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jin also became a model for the Manchus: Nurhaci, a leader of the <b>Jianzhou Jurchen</b>, named his state the Later Jin in 1616 before his successor renamed it Qing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Jianzhou Jurchens</b>, near the Mudan River, the Changbai mountains and Liaodong, hunted and fished, farmed and traded in pearls and ginseng, and were said to make clothes like the Chinese.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jurchens">Jurchens · Society</a></li></ul></section>
